					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/ghostchibi" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1382994); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1382994&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609233410" alt="ghostchibi" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1382994)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/ghostchibi" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1382994); return false;" >ghostchibi</a></span> <span class="odate time_1505848211 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">19 Sep 2017 19:10</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3597570">
									

<p>Part of this tale is an explanation about my author avatar's relation to meta-ness (because that's an inherent quality of them but there's not much a way to explain that relation to meta &quot;knowledge&quot; that they have without discussing myself the author, and that breaks the narrative ironically enough if I were to do that in the tales I'm planning to write using them) set up in a conversation about my continued failed attempts to write horror and sadness for the site (which is an old thing from my early days, and five years later I'm still trying to kick off the idea wormed into my head that somehow writing a bunch of cute animal scps isn't &quot;good&quot;). This isn't about dying on a hill or raging against the horror and sad writing, it's minor internal thing. Hence why the conversation happens between me and my author avatar, it's not meant to include anyone else. There's also an aspect of the character basically politely asking for whatever narratives I write to be as un-painful as possible, because for the character it's not fun to exist in a world of pain all the time.</p>
<p>The other part is a discussion about meta and writing; things like what exact document is being worked on, the music playing, the fact that Chibi's socks don't match, or the exact time of the moment don't actually have meaning unless given meaning to them. It doesn't matter exactly what any one aspect of the narrative is unless a reason for it is assigned. I meant it to tie into the instability of the narrative in the same line as the &quot;asking for less painful existences&quot; thing, where it makes life a lot harder for the characters when things aren't clear cut and have a reason. It's not meant to be conflict; a lot of writers (not just on the site, but in general) say &quot;if I my characters were real they'd probably kill me&quot; or something along those lines, and this was meant to be a reversal of that where character and creator don't have an antagonistic relationship.</p>
<p>The last line is calling back to Chibi saying &quot;I could absolutely just blank this document on my screen right now if I wanted to,&quot; in response to me the author pointing out that Chibi is busy only because I say they are. Chibi is busy because I said they were, and now I the author have removed that busy-ness from the narrative by just getting rid of whatever it was Chibi was working on. The &quot;&lt;3&quot; Chibi leaves is one last message in response to that bit of &quot;kindness&quot; so to speak.</p>

							</div>

								
					
